<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Living in the Truth" cic="CCC 2464-2513" scripture="Jn 8:32; Jn 14:6; Eph 4:25; Ex 20:16; Mt 5:37">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does the eighth commandment forbid, and what positive duty does it impose? (CCC 2464)</li>
<li>Why is Jesus Christ called "the Truth" (Jn 14:6), and what does that mean for how Christians must live?</li>
<li>What is the difference between detraction and calumny? Give a concrete example of each.</li>
<li>What is rash judgment and why does Jesus warn against it in Matthew 7:1-5?</li>
<li>Under what circumstances is a person obliged to keep a secret, and when must a secret be revealed?</li>
<li>What does the Catechism teach about reparation for offenses against truth? (CCC 2487)</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no "right" answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Read John 8:31-32. Jesus says the truth will set you free. When have you experienced this &mdash;that telling the truth, even when it was hard, brought you freedom? Or have you experienced the opposite: being trapped by a lie you told?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. The Catechism says that "the right to the communication of the truth is not unconditional" (CCC 2488). Have you ever been in a situation where telling the whole truth would have harmed someone unnecessarily? How did you navigate that tension between honesty and charity?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Think about your conversations this past week. Have you shared information about someone that they would not want shared (detraction)? Have you repeated something about another person without verifying whether it was true? What motivated you to say those things?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. St. Thomas More went to his death rather than swear a false oath. Is there a truth you are afraid to defend because of what it might cost you &mdash;socially, professionally, or in relationships? What would it take for you to have the courage to stand firm?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Social media makes it easy to share, comment, and judge instantly. Have you ever posted, shared, or commented something that you later realized was false, unfair, or damaging to someone's reputation? What would it look like to use digital media as an instrument of truth rather than a weapon?</p>
